<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<materials>

  <!--
    ================================================================
    GRAiNITA materials for DD4hep / Key4hep
    ================================================================

    GRAiNITA = ~1 mm ZnWO4 scintillator grains immersed in a heavy
    transparent liquid, 80% / 20% by MASS (ALFA deck, slide 90).

    Derived quantities (see README for the calculation):

      grain volume packing fraction   f     = 0.587
      effective density               rho   = 5.78 g/cm3
      effective radiation length      X0    = 1.60 cm
      effective Moliere radius        Rm    = 2.56 cm
      effective Z                     Zeff  = 51

    Cross-check: 25 X0 = 40.0 cm, which is exactly the depth of the
    17 x 17 x 40 cm3 demonstrator module quoted as "25 X0 in depth"
    on slide 94. This is the main validation that the mixture model
    is right.

    ================ OPEN INPUT, PLEASE VERIFY ================
    The deck says "sodium tungstate water solution". A plain Na2WO4
    solution saturates around 1.5 g/cm3, which is far too light to
    give the 80/20 mass split at a sensible grain packing fraction.
    Reaching ~2.8-3.1 g/cm3 requires sodium POLYtungstate,
    Na6(H2W12O40), the standard dense-media-separation heavy liquid.
    That is what is assumed below.

    Ask the IJCLab/LPClermont GRAiNITA people for the actual liquid
    and its density before using this for anything but a PoC. The
    sensitivity is mild but not negligible:

      rho_liq = 2.80  ->  f=0.587, rho_eff=5.78, X0=1.60 cm, Rm=2.56 cm
      rho_liq = 3.00  ->  f=0.604, rho_eff=5.94, X0=1.55 cm, Rm=2.50 cm
      rho_liq = 3.10  ->  f=0.612, rho_eff=6.02, X0=1.53 cm, Rm=2.47 cm

    The X0 in g/cm2 is almost identical for the grains (9.17) and the
    liquid (9.6), because both are tungstate-dominated. So the
    effective X0 in cm is driven almost entirely by rho_eff, i.e. by
    the packing fraction. That is the number worth pinning down.
    ================================================================
  -->

  <!-- ========== constituents ========== -->

  <material name="ZnWO4">
    <D type="density" unit="g/cm3" value="7.87"/>
    <composite n="1" ref="Zn"/>
    <composite n="1" ref="W"/>
    <composite n="4" ref="O"/>
  </material>

  <!-- Sodium polytungstate, Na6(H2W12O40), as the anhydrous solute -->
  <material name="SodiumPolytungstate">
    <D type="density" unit="g/cm3" value="4.00"/>
    <composite n="6"  ref="Na"/>
    <composite n="2"  ref="H"/>
    <composite n="12" ref="W"/>
    <composite n="40" ref="O"/>
  </material>

  <!-- Water, defined locally: DD4hep's DDDetectors materials.xml does not
       provide a "Water" material, and relying on it fails at load time.
       Named GrainitaWater to avoid clashing with any downstream definition. -->
  <material name="GrainitaWater">
    <D type="density" unit="g/cm3" value="1.00"/>
    <composite n="2" ref="H"/>
    <composite n="1" ref="O"/>
  </material>

  <!-- Heavy liquid: 85.7% SPT / 14.3% water by mass -> 2.80 g/cm3 -->
  <material name="GrainitaHeavyLiquid">
    <D type="density" unit="g/cm3" value="2.80"/>
    <fraction n="0.857" ref="SodiumPolytungstate"/>
    <fraction n="0.143" ref="GrainitaWater"/>
  </material>

  <!-- ========== the effective homogeneous medium ========== -->
  <!--
    This is what the full-detector simulation sees. It reproduces the
    shower development of the grain/liquid mixture correctly (X0, Rm,
    dE/dx, Zeff) but knows nothing about optical transport. Light
    collection is applied later, at digitisation.
  -->
  <material name="Grainita">
    <D type="density" unit="g/cm3" value="5.778"/>
    <fraction n="0.80" ref="ZnWO4"/>
    <fraction n="0.20" ref="GrainitaHeavyLiquid"/>
  </material>

  <!-- ========== WLS fibre ========== -->
  <!--
    Treated as passive in the effective model: it displaces active
    medium and is a source of non-uniformity, but its energy deposit
    is not collected. Polystyrene core; cladding ignored at PoC level.
  -->
  <material name="WLSFibreCore">
    <D type="density" unit="g/cm3" value="1.05"/>
    <composite n="8" ref="C"/>
    <composite n="8" ref="H"/>
  </material>
  <!-- ==========================================================
       Barrel medium: Grainita with the WLS fibre grid folded in
       ==========================================================

       In the test module the 625 fibres are placed explicitly. In a
       barrel there would be several million, which would make Geant4
       navigation miserable for no physics gain. So instead the fibres
       are homogenised into the medium:

         fibre volume fraction = pi*r^2/pitch^2 = pi*0.5^2/7^2 = 1.603%
         effective density     = 5.7022 g/cm3
         mass fractions        = 0.99705 Grainita / 0.00295 polystyrene

       This slightly dilutes the medium, so X0 grows from 1.595 cm to
       about 1.616 cm and 25 X0 becomes 40.4 cm rather than 39.9 cm.

       What this DOES capture: the average dead material of the fibre
       grid. What it does NOT capture: the position dependence of light
       collection between and along fibres, which is the source of the
       constant term. That belongs in the digitisation parametrisation,
       not the geometry.
  -->
  <material name="GrainitaBarrelMix">
    <D type="density" unit="g/cm3" value="5.7022"/>
    <fraction n="0.99705" ref="Grainita"/>
    <fraction n="0.00295" ref="WLSFibreCore"/>
  </material>

</materials>
